hey guys welcome back so last week I  posted a video of the famous tremolo  piece recuerdo Stella Alhambra and so  many of you reached out asking to see if  I would do a video talking about how I  play tremolo so here we are this video  will be in two parts if you are  guitarist who can't play tremolo but who  wants to learn this video is for you and  if you're a more advanced guitar player  but you want to learn how to improve  your tremolo technique next week there  will be a part two just for you so stay  tuned  [Music]  so tremolo on the guitar is like a magic  trick it creates the illusion that there  are two instruments playing at the same  time and the way it works is that  basically the thumb plays its own part  like this are simple arpeggiation and  then that's gonna keep on going but then  we're gonna add the fingers rapidly  plugging in between each thumb stroke  like this  [Music]  and it creates the most beautiful  haunting murmuring melody in the  background  [Music]  so to break that down further between  every thumb stroke we have to fit in  these three fingers in this order ring  middle index so tremolo is when we play  thumb ring middle index thumb ring  middle index on repeat now in classical  guitar or lingo we give the fingers  special names this is P I M and a so  Pema Pema whatever you want to call it  but for the rest of the video I'll be  referring to the fingers by those terms  okay so to learn this technique let's  start by putting all of our fingers in  our right hand except the pinky on the  first string that's the high E string  and make sure when you do this that your  thumb actually goes to the left of the  fingers so it's not inside the hand  hidden it's to the left of the fingers  from your point of view also the wrist  shouldn't be bent in like this sometimes  guitarists like to rest their palm on  the bridge or the wood this is no good  we want to keep the wrist relatively  straight some light natural bend is is  okay so all four fingers on the first  string thumb to the left straight wrist  and now we can start P a M I P  am i that's the basic tremolo technique  okay now once you practice this a bit we  can get ready for our first exercise  it's all about planting the fingers  early on the string so after you play P  the thumb the ring finger is going to  land instantly down on the string which  will stop it early and then when we play  the ring finger that middle finger M is  gonna land early then the middle finger  plays and their index lands early so all  the fingers land early which stops the  strings and makes it sound staccato  staccato simply means short in Italian  so here we go playing staccato tremolo  at 60 BPM and while you do this  try to keep your hands still we don't  want to play with the hands we don't  want to play with the arm keep the hand  nice and still  [Music]  and next we can alternate that staccato  exercise with a legato exercise legato  being the opposite of staccato if  staccato means short legato means  there's no gap between the notes they  are connected like so that's legato so  now we can go back and forth between the  two  once staccato once legato ready go  keep the hands still remember  [Music]  okay the next exercise is all about  learning to control the amount of force  that each finger exerts on the string  and the way we're going to do that is by  practicing accenting one finger at a  time so let's turn a metronome on at 80  bpm and we'll start by accenting P the  thumb ready and this turns out to be  pretty easy because the thumb is  naturally louder than the fingers but  next let's go to the a finger so we have  P a  hey am i next we'll do em em try to be  with the metronome  okay let's move to the index finger hi  [Music]  and lastly we're gonna go for a  completely even tremolo  [Music]  this is surprisingly difficult one  finger  inevitably wants to pop out so spend  some good time here and see if you can  make them really all sound the same okay  so now that your tremolo is working  let's go to the next level we want to  start letting the thumbs skip around  between different strings while the  fingers stay on the first string doing  tremolo this is what creates the  illusion of two instruments playing at  the same time so what we're going to do  is we'll start with all four fingers in  the first string and then one by one the  thumb will jump to the second string  third string fourth all the way to the  sixth string E and then back up for this  let's turn our metronome Jean to 110 bpm  ready  [Music]  remembering to keep your thumb to the  left  flat wrists  [Music]  okay now let's just have some fun with  this if you just hold down a basic chord  like a minor the left hand just leave  those fingers there and we're just going  to do basically the exercise we just did  but your thumb can skip around at random  it sounds like this  [Music]  instantly beautiful and if you want to  even go to the next level we can add a  chord progression to left hand let's try  a minor D minor E major  we'll finish back on a minor here we go  [Music]  okay last thing for the day let's learn  a little Spanish tune called Mel Agana  the whole thing is going to be played  with the thumb and it sounds like this  [Music]  and that's just going to repeat play  this until you can do it perfectly every  single time because next we're gonna add  tremolo between each thumb stroke  [Music]  and then you can go faster  [Music]  now go spend 10 minutes a day working on  these three exercises just like we did  today with the metronome and in a week  or so you'll already be able to start  incorporating this magical technique  into your playing and remember if you're  a more advanced player and want to go  deeper part two is next week we'll be  talking all about more advanced tremolo  techniques but for now thank you for  watching please subscribe if you haven't  already also you can consider supporting  my patreon if you want to help me make  more videos in the future  see you next time  [Music]  you
 

